**Type 1 diabetes** is a lifelong condition where your **immune system destroys the cells that make insulin**. Insulin is a hormone that lets sugar (glucose) move from your blood into your cells to be used for energy. Without it, sugar builds up in your blood.

It usually starts in **childhood or young adulthood**, but it can start at any age. It is **not** caused by diet or lifestyle.

## Symptoms: the 4 Ts

Diabetes UK uses the **4 Ts** to help people spot type 1 diabetes:

- **Toilet:** peeing more, especially at night, or bedwetting in a child who was dry
- **Thirsty:** being very thirsty
- **Tired:** feeling very tired
- **Thinner:** losing weight without trying

Other signs include blurred vision, thrush and cuts that heal slowly. Symptoms can come on quickly, over days or weeks.

{% callout type="danger" title="Get help the same day" %}
If you or your child have the 4 Ts, see a GP **the same day** or call **NHS 111**. Go to A&E or call 999 if there is also **being sick, tummy pain, breath that smells fruity, deep or fast breathing, or drowsiness**. These can be signs of **diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)**, which is life-threatening.
{% /callout %}

## Getting diagnosed

A finger-prick or blood test shows high blood sugar. If type 1 diabetes is suspected, you will usually be **referred to a specialist team the same day**, because treatment needs to start quickly.

## Treatment

Everyone with type 1 diabetes needs **insulin**, by injection or pump. You will learn to check your glucose, count carbohydrates and adjust your insulin. See [type 1 diabetes treatment](/conditions/physical/type-1-diabetes/treatment/).

## Your yearly checks

You should have these checks at least once a year, to catch any problems early:

- **HbA1c** blood test (your average blood sugar)
- **eye screening**
- **foot check**
- **kidney** tests
- **blood pressure** and **cholesterol**
